WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.669
Alaska is the largest US state.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:06.960
It is also the least densely populated.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:16.880
Among its great treasures the untouched wilderness
But much of that is now being destroyed.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.100
Wildfires are not a new phenomenon here, but
they are changing.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.130
They are becoming more frequent - and more
intense.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:30.140
So much so, that this year, local firefighters
here in Anderson were unable to get them under

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:37.570
control
“When a fire happens and it gets bigger

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:45.010
than the local unit can handle like here in
Anderson they have some local firefighters

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.180
but they are very small.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.420
So when the fire gets bigger what they can
control.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.579
They would call for outside help.”

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:55.510
And help has come from all over the US.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:01.460
Kate Airhart from Montana has been battling
blazes country-wide for more than 20 years.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.210
This is her fifth time in Alaska.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:10.170
She is now supervising a team of 500 firefighters
deployed to help control one wildfire…

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.639
Sadly, this property couldn’t be saved.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:20.470
„It’s really when fires like this one
start in and around populated areas even a

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:24.209
lightly populated area like this, that it
becomes a problem.”

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:31.569
“Year after year you hear that I’ve never
seen a fire behavior like this before.

00:01:31.569 --> 00:01:35.469
And so I would say this abnormal weather is
the new normal.

00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:41.700
It’s getting harder to fight fires, resources
are getting scarcer and we’re dealing with

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.700
winds like this”
This summer more than 264 individual fires

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.840
have destroyed 1.25 million hectares of land
across the state.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.350
The blaze in Anderson is now under control,
but the damage is extensive, and the strong

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:01.139
winds pose a risk too.

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:02.139
This used to be a forest.

00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:05.609
We can see still smoke up there.

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:08.990
The fire fighters are still trying to get
it under control.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:15.920
We can strongly smell it.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.860
Local people have had to evacuate their homes
– among them, Don and Dorothy.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.980
Winds were accelerating the blaze.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:39.641
“They took all the firefighters out because
it was so fast, it was a windy day, not as

00:02:39.641 --> 00:02:46.800
windy as today, but it was like 3-4 miles
it a few hours…”

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.720
When they returned, the fire was under control.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.330
“It was kind of surreal.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:59.310
There was smoke and fires in the trees over
there burning.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.120
The firemen were with us they said your house
is safe!”

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.880
This time they were lucky, but there will
certainly be wildfires here again, and experts

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.180
say global warming may make them more severe.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:16.030
This time they were lucky, but there will
certainly be wildfires here again, and experts,

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.460
like Prof Thoman say global warming may make
them more severe.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:24.070
“Lightning is the principal cause of wildfires
in Alaska.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:28.470
And so just a little bit of warming, just
a little bit of added moisture, more lightning

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:34.500
strikes on ground that is dryer than it used
to be because the snow is melting earlier,

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.170
giving it more chance to dry out.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.110
So, you’ve got a flammable landscape.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:44.560
we have this confluence of events, that are
being driven by the large scale warming”

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.420
“We’re getting more and more fires that
are just incinerators.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.560
They turn whole landscapes that look like
the moon.”

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.350
In the middle of the ashes hope remains, that
at least a part of this charred land might

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:01.349
return to life… until the next wildfires
hit again.

